      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To find the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\), we need to compute \\(5^{1993} \\mod 1000\\). We can use properties of modular arithmetic to simplify this calculation.\n\nFirst, observe that:\n\\[5^2 = 25\\]\n\\[5^4 = 625\\]\n\\[5^8 = 625^2 = 390625 \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000\\]\n\\[5^{16} = (5^8)^2 = 625^2 \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000\\]\n\nFrom this, we can see a pattern:\n\\[5^{16k} \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000\\]\n\\[5^{16k+2} \\equiv 25 \\mod 1000\\]\n\nSo, we need to express \\(5^{1993}\\) in the form \\(5^{16m+r}\\) where \\(r\\) is the exponent such that \\(1 \\leq r \\leq 15\\). We find \\(m\\) and \\(r\\) by performing the division:\n\\[1993 \\div 16 = 124 \\text{ remainder } 9\\]\nThis means \\(1993 = 16 \\cdot 124 + 9\\), so \\(5^{1993} = 5^{16 \\cdot 124 + 9} = (5^{16})^{124} \\cdot 5^9 \\equiv 625^{124} \\cdot 5^9 \\mod 1000\\).\n\nSince \\(5^{16} \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000\\), we have:\n\\[625^{124} \\equiv (625^{16})^{7} \\equiv 625^7 \\mod 1000\\]\n\nNow, we need to compute \\(625^7 \\mod 1000\\). Notice that:\n\\[625 = 625 \\equiv 25 \\mod 1000\\]\nSo,\n\\[625^7 \\equiv 25^7 \\mod 1000\\]\n\nWe can break down \\(25^7\\) using properties of modular arithmetic:\n\\[25^2 = 625 \\equiv 25 \\mod 1000\\]\n\\[25^4 = (25^2)^2 \\equiv 25^2 \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000\\]\n\\[25^6 = (25^4)^1.5 \\equiv (625)^{1.5} \\equiv 625 \\cdot \\sqrt{625} \\equiv 625 \\cdot 25 = 15625 \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000\\]\n\\[25^7 = 25^6 \\cdot 25 \\equiv 625 \\cdot 25 = 15625 \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000\\]\n\nThus,\n\\[625^7 \\equiv 625 \\mod 1000\\]\n\nSo,\n\\[5^{1993} \\equiv 625 \\cdot 5^9 \\mod 1000\\]\n\\[5^9 = 1953125 \\]\n\\[1953125 \\mod 1000 = 125\\]\n\\[625 \\cdot 125 = 78125\\]\n\\[78125 \\mod 1000 = 125\\]\n\nTherefore, the rightmost three digits of \\(5^{1993}\\) are \\(\\boxed{125}\\).",
